  "updated": "2026-07-21T12:02:54.280237+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong storms expected today and Wednesday.\n\nAn upper level shortwave trough looks to push through the Great\nLakes region, and an associated cold front will push into the\nOhio valley and Mid-Atlantic states this afternoon and evening.\nBoth of these mechanisms will provide ample forcing for ascent\nacross the region for widespread shower and thunderstorm\ndevelopment.\n\nWith the nose of a strong 250mb jet streak pushing into the\nregion from the northwest today, deep layer shear looks to be in\nthe 40-60 knot range through the afternoon and evening areas,\nespecially for areas along and north of I-64 where better shear\nwill be present. Forecast CAM soundings indicate that some low\nlevel clockwise turning hodographs look develop; however, with\nsouthwesterly flow at the surface today, the amount of clockwise\nturning looks to remain modest at best. The best shear and\nhodograph profiles look to arrive with the actual cold front\nthat may not arrive into the forecast area until after 00 UTC.\nAs the cold front arrives, winds look to back out of the\nsoutheast, which provides a much more impressive clockwise\nturning hodograph. The largest tornado threat will likely be\naccompanied by the approach and passage of the front; however,\nwith timing uncertainty, and possible lack of daytime heating,\nthe threat for any tornado will be dependent on all of these\ningredients coming together at once. CAM guidance does have a\nmessy convective evolution this afternoon and evening; however,\nmulticellular clusters, with a supercell or two can't be ruled\nout. Some multicellular clusters may have bowing line segments\nassociated with them given the amount of deep layer shear\npresent today. \n\nOverall the confidence in tornadoes and hail are low given the\nuncertainties mentioned above. The most impactful threat\nexpected for today is damaging wind gusts, which will lead to\nthe potential for numerous downed trees and power poles across\nthe area. This will likely inadvertently lead to the possibility\nfor more widespread power outages across the area given the more\nwidespread nature of storms expected today. \n\nIn terms of evolution, it looks like there could be two rounds\nof storms today, with early afternoon convection developing\nacross the mountains and pushing east, and then an additional\nround in the late evening as the cold front pushes into the\nregion. This second round looks to arrive at sunset or later for\nmountain locations. \n\nOn Wednesday, the aforementioned front looks to get hung up in a\neast/west orientation along the VA/NC border providing a focal\npoint for shower/thunderstorm activity primarily south of 460.\nSo far it appears that damaging winds and a tornado or two can't\nbe ruled out on the eastern side of this frontal boundary in the\nPiedmonts of VA/NC on Wednesday afternoon. Eventually, this\nfront looks to push south late Wednesday night, and by Thursday,\nenough dry air advecting into the area looks to end any rain \nchances for at least one day. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures should turn more seasonable by the\nmiddle of the week after a frontal passage.\n\nStrong summertime front passing through the region tonight will\nbring a large pattern change to the area starting late Wednesday\ninto Thursday. Behind the passage of the front, upper level \ntroughing looks to settle across the eastern CONUS through much \nof the work week. This will keep 500mb heights much lower across\nthe region leading to overall cooler temperatures. Surface high\npressure coming out of Canada will also lower dewpoints into \nthe 50s and 60s which will make it feel much more comfortable \noutside during the peak heat of the day. Highs look to only \nclimb into the upper 70s to low 80s across the mountains, and \nmid to upper 80s across the Piedmont starting Wednesday, and \ncontinuing through Saturday before temperatures look to increase\nagain on Sunday of next week. Shower and thunderstorm chances \nbeyond the frontal passage Tuesday night will primarily be \nrelegated to mountain locations, and look to remain isolated, \nwhere orographical lift can enhance development during peak \nheating. While chances look to be minimal on Thursday, they\nslowly start to increase on Friday and into the weekend.",
